    "storyBody": "Shulan had eighteen days left before the workshop assignment test. If she earned a place on the advanced machine, she would receive a fixed monthly allowance; if she failed, she would remain a temporary fill-in. Her speed on a full skirt was already among the best. Her cutting, waistband work, and pressing were steady. Only the final eight centimeters of an invisible zipper tended to ripple slightly. During rush production, the faint waves were hard to see after pressing, but the assignment test would inspect the zipper seam before pressing. Shulan assumed she simply had not done enough repetitions, so every evening after work she made two additional skirts from start to finish.\n\nA week later, she had completed fourteen extra skirts. Her average time for a whole garment had fallen from twenty-one minutes to nineteen, but the rework marks on the final eight centimeters had barely decreased. Shulan first suspected that the old machine was feeding unevenly and asked the master tailor to inspect it. He did not change machines. Using the same machine, the same roll of zipper, and Shulan’s leftover fabric, he stitched a section that lay flat on the table. Back at her own station, Shulan timed the full process for the first time. Of the nineteen minutes, she spent only seven seconds passing through those eight troublesome centimeters. Two extra skirts gave her almost forty more minutes of practice, but only fourteen additional seconds on the actual problem.\n\nThe next day, she stopped using full skirt panels. Instead, she cut a stack of twenty-centimeter scraps and drew a red line across the final eight centimeters of each piece. She lowered the machine speed at first. After each strip, she immediately laid it flat and placed a thin steel ruler beside it to see where the ripple began. The master tailor made only one mark on one sample: once she crossed the red line, her left hand began drawing the fabric edge outward too early. Shulan adjusted the movement and stitched six flat samples in a row at low speed. One speed setting higher, the fourth sample rippled again. That single correction had not solved the problem automatically. She put a red dot where the wave appeared, and on the next sample watched only the position of her hand and the feeding rhythm.\n\nFor the first two days, she tried to take advantage of the momentum and practised late into the night. In the later rounds the red dots multiplied, and her wrist began to ache. From the third day, she divided practice into twenty-five-minute rounds and left the machine between rounds to move her fingers. She increased the speed only after four flat samples in a row; if two samples rippled consecutively, she returned to the previous setting and checked where the first red dot appeared. At the end of each round, she clipped the flattest and most wrinkled samples side by side under the lamp. By the twelfth day, the stack of scrap fabric was thick. The red lines remained, but the red dots had gone from a string of marks to only a few scattered ones.\n\nOn the night before the test, the workshop received a rush order for formal dresses, and anyone who stayed late could earn double hourly pay. Shulan calculated the rent, but still finished only her normal shift and went home to sleep, giving up one night of overtime pay. The next day, the examiner first inspected an unpressed invisible-zipper sample and then allowed those who passed to begin the timed full garment. At the point corresponding to the red line, Shulan did not deliberately slow down; she fed the fabric at the speed she had already practised to stability. The sample lay flat and passed. She also completed the full skirt within the time limit, although she lost points for one corner of the waistband.\n\nThat afternoon, the assignment list was posted beside the door. Shulan had passed in fifth place. The first-place finisher had submitted nine minutes earlier, and two others had higher waistband scores. She did not take the entire stack of practice scraps with her. She pulled out the last piece and the first one, held them together for a few seconds, then cut off the section marked by the eight-centimeter red line and tucked it beneath the needle box on her new machine.",
    "storyBodyZh": "舒兰还有十八天参加车间定岗考核。拿到高级机台，她每月能多一笔固定津贴；没通过，就继续做临时补位。她做整条裙子的速度已经排在前面，裁片、腰头和熨烫都很稳，只有隐形拉链最后八厘米常会微微起浪。平时赶货时，轻微波纹经过熨烫不太显眼，但定岗考核会在熨烫前检查拉链缝。舒兰觉得自己只是做得还不够多，每天下班后再完整缝两条裙子。\n\n一周后，她额外做完十四条，整件平均时间从二十一分钟降到十九分钟，最后八厘米的返工记号却几乎没少。舒兰先怀疑旧机台送料不匀，便请师傅替她看机器。师傅没有换机，只用同一台、同一卷拉链和她剩下的布料缝了一段，摊在桌上是平的。舒兰回到自己的机位，第一次拿秒表看完整流程：十九分钟里，她真正经过那八厘米只有七秒。两条额外裙子让她多练了近四十分钟，却只多遇到那个问题十四秒。\n\n第二天，她没再拿整片裙料，而是剪出一叠二十厘米长的废布，在每块最后八厘米处划一道红线。她先把机速降下来，每缝完一条就立刻摊平，与薄钢尺并排看波纹从哪一针开始。师傅只在其中一块旁边做了一个记号：进入红线后，她左手会提前向外带布。舒兰照着改，慢速时连续缝平了六块；机速提高一档，第四块又起浪。那一个手势没有自动解决问题，她把红点标在起浪的位置，下一块只盯手的位置和送料节奏。\n\n最初两天，她想趁热多练，做到后半夜时红点反而越来越密，手腕也开始发酸。第三天起，她把练习切成二十五分钟一轮，中间离开机台活动手指。连续四块平整才提高一档；若两块连续起浪，就退回上一档，检查红点第一次出现在哪里。每轮结束，她把最平和最皱的两块夹在灯下比较。到第十二天，那叠废布已经很厚，红线仍在，红点却从一串变成零星几个。\n\n考核前夜，车间接到一批加急礼服，留下加班的人能拿双倍时薪。舒兰算了算房租，还是只做完正常班次就回去睡觉，少拿了一晚加班费。第二天考官先检查一段未熨烫的隐形拉链，再让合格者进入整件限时制作。舒兰在红线对应的位置没有刻意拖慢，只按自己已经稳定练到的速度送布；样片平整通过。整件裙子也按时完成，不过腰头一个转角被扣了分。\n\n下午，定岗名单贴在门边。舒兰合格，排第五；第一名比她早交九分钟，另有两个人的腰头分数比她高。她没有把那叠练习布全带走，只抽出最后一块和第一块叠在一起看了几秒，然后剪下那段八厘米红线，压在新机台的针盒下面。",
    "explanation": "Deliberate practice targets a clearly identified weak component with focused attention, usable feedback, error correction, and repeated attempts slightly beyond the current level of reliable performance. It differs from repeatedly performing an entire familiar routine, which may preserve fluency while continuing to hide errors. The exact form varies by domain, and rest, coaching, motivation, and opportunity also shape improvement.",
    "explanationZh": "刻意练习针对明确的薄弱成分，以集中注意、可用反馈、错误修正和略高于当前稳定水平的反复尝试促成改进。它不同于不断重复整套熟悉表现；后者可能维持流畅，却继续掩盖错误。具体做法因领域而异，休息、指导、动机和机会也会影响进步。"
