    "module": {
      "intro": "The key change in the story is not that Xiaomo finally encounters a bird he likes. It is that both participants begin using pointing, touch, and picture cards to bring the other person to the same focus, and both can initiate and follow. Joint attention lies in this coordination, not in the attractiveness of a particular object or the appearance of a new word.",
      "diagramTitle": "How joint attention moves from one-sided attention to a shared focus",
      "diagramSteps": [
        "One person notices an object or event and sends an observable invitation to attend",
        "The other person follows the invitation into the same focus",
        "The initiator uses a further action to confirm that the other person has joined",
        "Both participants can initiate or follow later shifts of attention",
        "The shared focus creates opportunities for communication and learning"
      ],
      "diagramStepNotes": [
        "The object may be a bird, a red strand, a picture card, or another event; the mechanism does not depend on the object itself being especially interesting.",
        "An invitation can be made through pointing, touch, moving an object, or sound. It does not require one fixed form, and eye contact is not necessary.",
        "The important point is not that two people happen to see the same thing, but that one person’s action changes the other person’s attention and receives an observable response.",
        "On both Wednesday and Friday, the teacher initiates and the child follows, and the child also initiates and the teacher follows. The interaction is therefore not simply the teacher accommodating the child’s interest.",
        "Words appearing within a shared interaction are only a possible learning opportunity. The appearance of three target words does not by itself prove that joint attention has produced vocabulary learning."
      ],
      "mapping": [
        "Xiaomo touches Xu’s sleeve and points to the red strand → he actively invites her into his focus of attention.",
        "Xu follows his pointing, finds the red strand, and points to the same place → her response makes the shared focus observable.",
        "Xu later points first to the red strand or the sparrow, and Xiaomo follows her gesture → the teacher can initiate and the child can follow.",
        "Xiaomo places the string card below where Xu is pointing and later uses the bird card to bring her back to the sparrow → objects help the two of them continue coordinating the shared focus.",
        "Not all planned words appear during the observation → joint attention remains distinct from vocabulary attainment, and a single language outcome does not replace the mechanism itself."
      ],
      "boundary": "Joint attention is not forced eye contact, and it is not simply two people looking at the same object by chance. Different children may coordinate attention through different actions, sounds, or objects. Joint attention can support language and social communication, but the story cannot prove that a particular word has been learned, and interest in one object should not itself be treated as evidence of joint attention.",
      "reflection": "If Xu had simply allowed Xiaomo to keep watching the birds, without either of them initiating and following the other, what part of joint attention would still be missing?"
    },
