    "module": {
      "intro": "A-Qin and Minya both face legitimate timing pressure: there are only two hours from 1:00 to 3:00 to prepare the memorial, and only twenty minutes after the 5:00 ending before the dinner team arrives. The key change is not simply that “the table is reserved.” Before the boundary forms, the same surface still supports setup work. Once the white cloth, photographs, arriving families, name-reading, and floor line are all in place, the entire table and its immediate area stop serving backstage purposes even when some space is physically empty. When the memorial ends, those arrangements are withdrawn one by one.",
      "diagramTitle": "How a community changes the social status of the same object through ritual time and rules of use",
      "diagramSteps": [
        "During ordinary setup, the same object still supports several everyday tasks",
        "The white cloth, photographs, arriving families, and name-reading gradually establish a boundary of separation",
        "Once the boundary forms, physical emptiness no longer automatically means free use",
        "Other legitimate needs continue through alternative spaces rather than crossing the current boundary",
        "After the ritual ends, photographs, cloth, and floor markings are removed and ordinary uses return"
      ],
      "diagramStepNotes": [
        "Between 1:00 and 2:20, the long table is used for cutting name cards, holding water, and testing photo placement, showing that the boundary does not exist simply because the event has been booked.",
        "Once the white cloth is spread, photographs are arranged in name-reading order, families gather, and the floor line is taped down, the rules of use around the table visibly change.",
        "At 4:30, the far end of the table has no photographs, yet the kitchen still does not use it for sorting plates; this rules out simple explanations based only on lack of space or hygiene.",
        "A-Qin uses two smaller tables for dinner sorting, showing that the pressure of ordinary work remains real and is handled through alternative arrangements rather than by denying it.",
        "After the final name, the photographs are collected, the cloth is folded, and the floor line is removed; plates and playing cards then return to the same surface, showing the boundary being materially withdrawn."
      ],
      "mapping": [
        "Tools, water, and photographs share the table before 1:30 → before the memorial boundary is fully formed, the table still supports ordinary preparation work.",
        "The white cloth, family photographs, flowers, and ordered reading of names → concrete material practices that organize the table as the center of the memorial.",
        "The floor line and the unused corner still not serving backstage work → the classification changes the whole field of acceptable action, not merely the amount of occupied surface or a cleanliness requirement.",
        "The kitchen uses two smaller tables instead → ordinary work continues but is spatially rerouted around the current boundary.",
        "After folding the cloth, removing the stands, and lifting the floor line, plates and cards return → the social status of the same object is reorganized with ritual time."
      ],
      "boundary": "Sacred/profane is not a static binary maintained in exactly the same way across all cultures, and sacred does not necessarily mean good, pure, or supernatural. Later scholarship emphasizes that such classifications are shaped by history, community, power, material practice, and contestation. This story shows only how one annual memorial in a contemporary multicultural community temporarily reorganizes the use of one long table and the space around it; it cannot stand for all religious, commemorative, or cultural practices.",
      "reflection": "At 4:30, the far end of the long table is physically empty. Why does the dinner team still use two smaller tables, while after 5:20 the plates can return directly to the long table?"
    },
