    "module": {
      "intro": "Marta and Thomas share the same goal: late arrivals should not be left outside the common meal. One applies “wait for one another” by delaying the whole meal; the other initially proposes beginning at 6:15 and leaving six meals in the kitchen. The ten-year-old records first weaken the idea that waiting for the last person is historically unavoidable. Then the surrounding context in 1 Corinthians 11—division, some eating first, others going hungry—changes Thomas’s proposal in a concrete way: from food held in the kitchen to reserved seats in the hall with someone joining the late arrivals at the table.",
      "diagramTitle": "How a community brings text, inherited practice, present conditions, and consequences into a testable interpretation",
      "diagramSteps": [
        "A present situation creates tension within an inherited practice",
        "The community checks the concrete conditions in which the older practice developed",
        "The scriptural prompt is returned to its wider textual context",
        "Different applications are compared against the community concern they are meant to serve",
        "A provisional practice, time boundary, and method for further testing are recorded publicly"
      ],
      "diagramStepNotes": [
        "6:15, 6:35, and 7:30 create a real scheduling pressure, and both initial proposals are trying to keep people from being excluded from the shared meal.",
        "The ten-year-old records show that the earlier rule emerged when most participants had already arrived before 6:00, so the parish tradition still matters but cannot simply be treated as a clock time mandated by the text itself.",
        "Rereading 1 Corinthians 11 is not an attempt to equate ancient Corinth with the modern dinner; it brings the passage’s concern with division, eating first, and others going hungry back into the present judgment.",
        "The contextual reading materially changes Thomas’s workflow note—from “keep six meals in the kitchen” to “reserve six seats in the hall, keep six meals warm, and have someone eat with the late shift”—so the text is not merely decorative.",
        "One dinner covers only one arrival pattern. The next month’s rota has a wider finish window, so the group extends the arrangement to three months and writes both the date and trial period on the back of the card."
      ],
      "mapping": [
        "The old card reading “Wait for one another (1 Cor. 11:33)” → Scripture has long functioned as a brief normative prompt within the parish’s common-meal practice rather than as a disguised claim of exact English wording from the whole verse.",
        "Most people arriving before 6:00 ten years earlier → “wait until everyone arrives” was once a reasonable practice under specific conditions, but not a fixed time requirement built into the text.",
        "The discussion of division, eating first, and others going hungry in 1 Corinthians 11 → the isolated prompt is returned to a wider textual relationship.",
        "“Keep six meals in the kitchen” becomes reserved seats in the hall with someone eating alongside the late arrivals → textual context and present circumstances jointly change the concrete application rather than producing a scheduling compromise alone.",
        "The date, 6:15 arrangement, and three-month trial written on the back → the new practice states its basis and boundary publicly while remaining open to further experience and revision."
      ],
      "boundary": "Theological hermeneutics does not mean denying that texts can have determinate meaning, nor does it mean that individuals may assign any meaning they wish. Different religions, denominations, and interpretive traditions have different canons, authorities, and interpretive norms. This story shows only how one contemporary Catholic parish uses 1 Corinthians 11 as a normative reference for a common meal while acknowledging that an ancient Corinthian gathering and a modern Friday parish dinner are not the same historical or liturgical setting. The three-month trial is not presented as a single eternal answer, but as a present judgment with publicly stated reasons that remains open to further testing.",
      "reflection": "If the card by the door were only decoration, why would rereading 1 Corinthians 11 lead Thomas to cross out “keep six meals in the kitchen” and replace it with reserved seats in the hall and someone eating with the late arrivals?"
    },
