    "storyBody": "At 8:00 on Monday morning, Tang Jing taught her first morning movement class in the courtyard shared by the residential building and community center. The property manager had already allowed residents and community programs to book the courtyard during the day. Tang Jing had previously rented a small indoor room by the hour; now she reduced the price per class from €12 to €6. The Monday and Wednesday sessions filled quickly, the waiting list kept growing, and she agreed to add a Friday morning class the following week.\n\nOn the third floor, Zhou Ning worked remote overnight shifts as a medical interpreter, starting before dawn and finishing at 9:00, often with one final call at 8:30. He had not enrolled in the class and did not know most of the participants. During the first two mornings, he could finish his shift by closing the windows and wearing noise-cancelling headphones. When the Friday session was added, the drums and the footsteps of more than twenty people again fell into the same working hour. At 8:10, he closed his laptop, packed his bag, and went to a coworking space around the corner.\n\nA sound-insulated booth there cost €12. The following Wednesday and Friday, he did not wait for the noise to start; he booked the same booth in advance, left home at 7:50, and moved the final hour of his existing night shift there. Tang Jing, meanwhile, still had a waiting list for all three classes. The €6 price covered the costs she could see—her time and speaker batteries—and the courtyard carried no rent. She even began asking whether a Thursday session could be added.\n\nAt the end of the month, the coworking space combined Zhou Ning’s three bookings into one €36 bill. He did not ask for the classes to stop. He sent the bill, his night-shift schedule, and a note asking whether they could find a way to coexist to the property manager. The manager forwarded the email to Tang Jing. When she opened the attachment, her own course sheet still said “courtyard rent: €0,” beside three full class lists. The €36 was not a total of every neighbor’s loss, but it was one third-party expense that had been completely absent from her accounts.\n\nThey first tried the option that changed the class schedule least. The next Monday, every participant wore headphones, Tang Jing stopped broadcasting spoken instructions, and the stepping area was moved toward the center of the courtyard. Zhou Ning stayed home and continued his normal night shift. The sound was much lower, but the dull thud of more than twenty people landing at once still reached his 8:30 call. That afternoon, Tang Jing booked the Wednesday and Friday classes back into the small indoor room and left Monday in the courtyard.\n\nWhen the new schedule went up, Monday still read “Courtyard 08:00,” while Wednesday and Friday now said “Small room.” The two indoor sessions were raised to €9, and the waiting list became shorter. Zhou Ning cancelled two coworking bookings for the following week. Tang Jing added a new line to her fee sheet—“indoor room rent”—with a printed copy of the €36 bill clipped beside it.",
    "storyBodyZh": "周一早上八点，唐静在住宅与社区中心共用的内院上第一节晨间律动课。物业已经允许住户和社区项目在白天预约院子；她原来租室内小厅，每小时要付场地费，现在把单次价格从十二欧降到六欧。周一、周三两场很快满员，候补名单还在增长，她答应下周再加一个周五早晨。\n\n三楼的周宁做远程夜班医疗口译，凌晨开始，早上九点结束，八点半往往还有最后一段连线。他没有报名课程，也不认识大多数学生。头两个早晨，他关窗、戴降噪耳机还能把班上完；周五第三场第一次出现时，鼓点和二十多人的踏步又落在同一工作时段，他在八点十分合上电脑，背包去了街角的共享办公室。\n\n那天的隔音工位十二欧。下一周三、周五，他不再等声音起来才决定，而是提前订了同一个位置，七点五十分出门，把本来就存在的夜班最后一小时搬到那里。唐静这边，三场都有人候补，六欧仍能覆盖她看得见的时间和音响电池成本；院子没有租金，她甚至开始询问周四是否还能增加一场。\n\n月底，共享办公室把三次预约合成一张36欧账单。周宁没有要求停课，只把账单、自己的夜班排班和一句“能不能找个共存办法”一起发给物业。管理员随后把邮件转给唐静。她打开附件时，自己的课程表正写着“院子租金：0”，旁边是三场满员名单。36欧不是所有邻居损失的总数，却是她此前账目里完全看不到的一笔第三方支出。\n\n他们先试了最少改变课程的办法。下一周周一，学员全部改戴耳机，唐静不再外放口令，踏步区也缩到院子中间。周宁留在家里继续原来的夜班；声音少了许多，可二十多个人同时落脚的闷响仍会进入八点半的通话。当天午后，唐静把周三、周五两场订回室内小厅，周一继续留在院子。\n\n新课表贴出时，周一仍写“院子 08:00”，周三、周五后面改成“小厅”，室内两场价格调到九欧，候补名单短了一截。周宁取消下一周两次共享办公室预约。唐静在收费表上新增“室内厅租金”一行，旁边夹着那张36欧账单打印件。",
    "explanation": "A negative externality occurs when part of the cost of an activity falls on a third party outside the transaction and is not fully included in the private price facing the decision-maker. Lower private cost can support a lower price or a higher level of activity.\n\nHarm alone does not automatically make something an externality. The key is that the affected third party did not take part in the transaction; one bill can also show only part of a visible burden, not precisely measure the full social cost.",
    "explanationZh": "负外部性指一项活动把部分成本落在交易之外的第三方身上，而这部分成本没有充分进入决策者面对的私人价格。较低的私人成本可能支持更低价格或更多活动。\n\n有损害并不自动构成外部性。关键是第三方未参与相关交易；某张账单也只能显示一部分可见成本，不等于精确计算全部社会成本。"
