    "title": "The Sixty-Day Card Behind the Invoice",
    "summary": "Cheng Mo worries that a rebuilt wheel may send him back to the workshop a few weeks later. The quotes differ by €6, and one comes with a sixty-day service card.",
    "storyParagraphs": [
      "Cheng Mo’s commuter-bike rear wheel starts wobbling again. He pushes it to two neighboring workshops for rebuilding quotes: €78 at Shop A, €72 at Shop B. Either can finish before Monday’s commute. The freshly adjusted wheels on their stands look straight. What worries him is taking time off to return a few weeks later, and he cannot tell at purchase which wheel will stay steadier.",
      "When writing the quote, Shop A’s technician takes a sixty-day service card from a drawer: one free re-truing if needed under normal commuting use. Cheng Mo asks whether it costs extra. It is included in the €78 and redeemed under those commuting terms. The technician fills in an order identifier, expiry date, and booking number, leaving the wheel number until collection. A duplicate stays at the shop, so a return visit will not require explaining the arrangement again to a different person.",
      "After closing, Shop A’s owner circles a return slot in next week’s booking sheet. Its full rebuilding process allows an extra half-day for rechecking, and its internal schedule budgets one return per ten orders. Adjustment uses no replacement parts, and wages are already paid by the shift. With bookings currently full, a free half-hour displaces an €18 paid adjustment. The owner crosses out that saleable slot and clips ten service-card duplicates behind the sheet.",
      "Next door, Shop B is considering the same promise. Its urgent rebuilding process leaves out one later recheck, and its internal scheduling budget allows five returns per ten orders. Both shops are busy; Shop B also uses no additional adjustment parts and pays wages by the shift. An extra €6 per order would require giving up five €18 slots. The owner keeps the €72 quote and attaches a booking sheet for separately paid aftercare.",
      "Cheng Mo knows neither shop’s planning budget. Returning the next day, he asks Shop A whether it will still honor the card if the wheel needs adjustment within sixty days. The technician shows him the duplicate and confirms that the shop will arrange the slot. Cheng Mo initially treated it as a service extra. Now the willingness to carry return work on the shop’s own account seems a clue worth considering. He chooses Shop A and saves its number.",
      "On Saturday, the technician adds the wheel number to the card and has Cheng Mo sign its duplicate, then staples his copy behind the €78 invoice. Cheng Mo saves the booking number, puts the invoice in his bike bag, and pushes the bike over the threshold. The technician files the matching duplicate in the clear pocket behind next week’s booking sheet. The return slot that could have been sold is still circled."
    ],
    "overall": "Signaling occurs under information asymmetry when an informed party voluntarily chooses an observable, enforceable action that leads the other side to update a judgment about hidden quality. The action can be informative when its expected cost or net benefit differs across potential types.",
    "conceptName": "Signaling",
    "explanationParagraphs": [
      "The scheduling figures are assumed: with the same extra €6 per order, one or five returns per ten orders displace €18 or €90 in paid slots. The two hidden processes therefore carry different expected slot costs for the same promise.",
      "The buyer cannot see those budgets, but can observe a numbered commitment and how to redeem it, using them to make a limited update about expected return work. The promise also has service value and can be imitated; buying is not proof of quality. This is not a complete profit or equilibrium model."
    ],
    "module": {
      "intro": "A credible signal must be observable and enforceable, and its expected cost or net benefit must differ across possible hidden types.",
      "diagramTitle": "How a €6 Service Difference Can Carry a Future Workshop-Slot Commitment",
      "diagramSteps": [
        "Start with wheel stability that the buyer cannot directly observe at purchase",
        "Have the seller voluntarily add a sixty-day free-adjustment commitment",
        "Map that commitment to a real paid workshop slot",
        "Compare expected commitment cost under different return frequencies",
        "Let the buyer update the decision while preserving other preferences and uncertainty"
      ],
      "diagramStepNotes": [
        "A newly rebuilt wheel does not reveal whether it will loosen again after several weeks.",
        "Shop A writes one free adjustment within 60 days, plus the wheel number, expiry, and booking path, into the order.",
        "The story assumes full bookings, no extra adjustment parts, and wages fixed by the shift; each free half-hour displaces an €18 paid adjustment.",
        "For ten orders, the same €6 premium adds €60; one return displaces €18, five displace €90. This compares only the promise’s expected slot cost.",
        "After checking the promise and duplicate, Cheng Mo treats the shop’s willingness to carry return work as a clue. It also has service value and is not proof of quality."
      ],
      "mapping": [
        "Sixty-day service card → a voluntary observable commitment by the seller.",
        "€18 adjustment slot → the real opportunity cost when the promise is redeemed.",
        "€6 price difference → added revenue toward the promise, without calculating all rebuilding costs or total profit.",
        "Different wheel-building processes → hidden differences related to expected short-term return work."
      ],
      "boundary": "Return budgets, full bookings, and fixed wages are story assumptions, not measured craft data. The figures compare only expected slot costs, not all costs or profits. Other types can imitate the card or offer it for reasons such as reputation; high-quality shops can decline it. Buyers must also consider whether it can be honored, and cannot infer certain quality or a market equilibrium from one card.",
      "reflection": "If the same free commitment only occasionally consumes a slot for one process but frequently consumes a slot for another, why might it carry information? Under what conditions would that information be weak?"
    },
