    "explanation": "Base-rate reasoning combines a test result with pretest probability—the expected prevalence of the condition in the relevant population. Even a fairly accurate test can yield many false positives when the condition is rare because the unaffected group is much larger. In the illustrative story, among 10,000 people with 0.1% prevalence, 90% sensitivity yields about nine true positives; specificity rounded to 98.9% yields about 111 false positives. Predictive values change with prevalence; likelihood ratios or Bayes' theorem formalize the update. Screening results therefore require context and, when appropriate, confirmation rather than automatic diagnosis.",
